The '''2005 FIFA Club World Championship''' (officially known as the '''FIFA Club World Championship Toyota Cup Japan 2005''' for sponsorship reasons) was the second [[FIFA Club World Championship]], a [[association football|football]] competition organised by [[FIFA]] for the champion clubs of the six continental confederations. It was the first to be held after by the merger between the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]] and the FIFA Club World Championship (which had been played in a first edition in 2000).

The tournament was held in Japan from 11 to 18 December 2005 and won by Brazilian club [[São Paulo FC|São Paulo]], who defeated English side [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] 1–0 in the [[2005 FIFA Club World Championship Final|final]].

This would be the only edition of the tournament until the [[2022 FIFA Club World Cup|2022 edition]] where the [[CONCACAF]] representative didn't come from [[Mexico]].

==Background==
The 2005 tournament was created as a merger between the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]] and the earlier [[FIFA Club World Championship]]s. The previous of these had been running as an annual tournament between the champions of Europe and South America since 1960; the latter had undergone just one tournament, the [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship]]. The [[2001 FIFA Club World Championship|2001]] tournament had been cancelled when FIFA's marketing partner [[International Sport and Leisure|ISL]] went bankrupt. To celebrate the marriage between the two competitions, a new trophy was introduced by [[FIFA]].

As a result of this merger, the tournament was conceived as being smaller than the original Club World Championship, which had lasted two weeks, yet building on the one game format of the Intercontinental Cup. Six clubs were invited to take part in the tournament, one representing each regional football confederation. The competition's name, which was the simple union between the name of the two previous merging competitions, was evidently too long, and was going to be reduced the following year, becoming the [[FIFA Club World Cup]].

==Format==
The competition was a [[Single-elimination tournament|knockout tournament]] so each team played two or three matches. The champions of the four "weaker" confederations played in the quarter-finals; the losers played in a fifth place play-off. The winners were then joined by the European and South American champions in the semi-finals; the losers played in a third place play-off.

The matches were held in Tokyo's National (Olympic) Stadium, Toyota Stadium in [[Toyota, Aichi|Toyota]], [[Aichi]], near [[Nagoya, Aichi|Nagoya]] and the [[International Stadium Yokohama|International Stadium]] in [[Yokohama]], where the final was played. For marketing purposes it was known as the ''FIFA Club World Championship Toyota Cup''.

==Qualified teams==
It was all six clubs' first appearance in the FIFA Club World Championship.

